{"qid":"Q431575","names":["Idrisids"],"title":"Idrisids","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Idrisid dynasty was the first Arab-Islamic dynasty to rule Morocco, founded by Idris ibn Abdallah, a descendant of Ali ibn Abi Talib who fled the Abbasid massacre at Fakhkh in 786 and was recognized as imam by the Awraba Berber confederation in 788. His son Idris II (r. 803–828) founded the city of Fez in 808, which became a center of Islamic learning and Maghrebi culture. The Idrisids fractured the realm among multiple heirs after Idris II, producing competing branches that weakened central authority; the Fatimids and Umayyads of Córdoba competed for influence over these successor principalities. The dynasty effectively ended when Fatimid forces under the Kutama Berbers and their allies absorbed the last Idrisid territories around 974–980.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Idrisids' core control of northern Morocco and the founding of Fez are well-attested; the dynasty's territorial reach into the Atlas and southern regions is poorly defined and dependent on Berber tribal loyalties rather than formal administration.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Idrisid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idrisid_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Idrisid dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Idrisid-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Idris I of Morocco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idris_I_of_Morocco"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Fez, Morocco — History","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fez,_Morocco"}],"britannica":"topic/Idrisid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Idrisid dynasty","rulers":[{"